          "text": "# MCG 1-32-100: A Far Island in Virgo\n\n*An elongated galaxy hides in plain sight among the spring constellations. Find it with a telescope, and its faint glow becomes a reminder that even a small patch of sky can hold a whole galaxy.*\n\nSomewhere in Virgo, a soft oval of light waits beyond the reach of unaided eyes. Catalogued as MCG 1-32-100 and also known as NGC 4526, it offers no dramatic flash or sharply defined spiral arms in a small telescope. Its appeal is quieter: a distant galaxy, broad enough to span several arcminutes, set against a sky crowded with other members of the Virgo galaxy region.\n\nNGC 4526\u2019s coordinates are about right ascension 12h 34m and declination +7\u00b0 42\u2032 (J2000). The SIMBAD database describes it as a galaxy toward a cluster of galaxies and lists an optical size of roughly 9.6 by 3.3 arcminutes. That elongated outline gives observers a useful expectation: look for a small, diffuse oval, not a point of light. SIMBAD lists its B-band magnitude as 10.6; that measurement is in blue light, so it should not be treated as a direct prediction of how bright the galaxy will look through an eyepiece.\n\nThe database gives a measured redshift of 0.001876, corresponding to a heliocentric radial velocity of about 562 kilometres per second. Redshift is the stretching of light as an object moves away; astronomers use it to study the motions and distances of galaxies. But nearby galaxy clusters complicate a simple distance estimate from that number: galaxies also move within the cluster, so their individual velocities do not reflect only the expansion of the universe.\n\n**Finding the faint oval**\n\nVirgo is a rewarding spring target for Northern Hemisphere observers, when it climbs high in the evening sky. A GoTo mount can be aimed at the coordinates above. If you star-hop, begin with a detailed atlas or planetarium chart: the galaxy lies in a rich region where several faint galaxies can appear close together, and a